\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

When multiple LLM judges evaluate model responses on a benchmark, their scores are shaped by at least three forces: the model's genuine ability, the judge's own scoring tendency, and any advantage the model gained from encountering benchmark items during training. These three signals are entangled in every observation. The evaluation community has developed separate tools for the latter two distortions: judge bias correction methods calibrate inter-judge differences \citep{cyclicjudge2026, uda2026}, while contamination detection tools flag or adjust for memorized items \citep{constat2024, dcr2025, logprober2024}. A practitioner who faces both problems must apply these corrections sequentially. This paper shows that sequential correction produces systematically biased rankings, not merely noisy ones.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig1_framework_v2}
\caption{(a)~Sequential pipelines address judge bias and contamination independently; omitted variable bias confounds the unmodeled facet into ability estimates. (b)~G-MFRM three-stage joint calibration: Stage~1 fits a standard MFRM for initial estimates $(\hat{\theta}, \hat{b}, \hat{d})$; Stage~2 estimates per-item exposure $e_i$ from external evidence (reference model anchoring, cross-model agreement, and optionally LogProber); Stage~3 performs joint MCMC inference with $e_i$ fixed, recovering contamination-corrected ability $\hat{\theta}_n$, contamination susceptibility $c_n$, and judge severity $d_j$.}
\label{fig:framework}
\end{figure*}

We formalize this failure as an omitted variable bias (OVB) problem \citep{angrist2009mostly}. Consider a model that has memorized some benchmark items and a judge that scores more strictly than average. A bias-only correction adjusts for the strict judge but leaves the contamination signal untouched; the inflated scores on memorized items get absorbed into the model's estimated ability. Conversely, a contamination-only adjustment removes the memorization signal but conflates systematic scoring differences with contamination variation. In both cases, the omitted facet biases the modeled one. Within the Many-Facet Rasch Model (MFRM) framework \citep{linacre1989mfrm}, we prove this bias is directional: contaminated models are systematically overestimated under bias-only correction, and the bias persists regardless of which correction comes first (Section~\ref{sec:confounding}).

Resolving this confounding requires joint estimation. We propose the Gated Many-Facet Rasch Model (G-MFRM), which extends MFRM with a multiplicative gating term $c_n \cdot e_i$ for contamination susceptibility, inspired by Doubly-Gated Item Response Theory \citep{shu2013dgirt}. Through a three-stage calibration procedure with externally anchored per-item exposure indicators (Figure~\ref{fig:framework}), G-MFRM simultaneously recovers model ability, item difficulty, judge severity, and contamination susceptibility. We prove that external anchoring of exposure is both necessary and sufficient for parameter identification under standard multi-judge designs (Theorem~\ref{thm:sufficient}), and derive error propagation bounds for the staged estimation.

In synthetic experiments ($N = 50$ models, 20 seeds), G-MFRM reduces ability RMSE by 44\% relative to sequential estimation ($p < 0.001$) and recovers contamination susceptibility with Pearson $r = 0.81$. On Arena-Hard \citep{li2024arenahard} with 6 LLM judges and 17 target models, G-MFRM produces calibrated per-model contamination diagnostics---older models (GPT-3.5-Turbo, GPT-4-0613) exhibit 4--6$\times$ higher susceptibility than recent models (GPT-4o)---and a controlled coverage ablation traces over-correction to incomplete exposure estimation ($\Delta\tau$: $-0.044 \to -0.015 \to 0.000$ as coverage increases from 46\% to 100\%). Leave-one-out cross-validation strongly favors joint estimation ($|\Delta\mathrm{ELPD}/\mathrm{SE}| = 5.9$; stacking weight 1.0). Our contributions are:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item A formal analysis of judge-contamination confounding as omitted variable bias, proving that sequential correction cannot resolve the confounding under non-orthogonality (Section~\ref{sec:confounding}).
    \item The G-MFRM framework with identifiability guarantees and error propagation bounds for staged estimation (Section~\ref{sec:method}).
    \item Empirical validation on Arena-Hard yielding calibrated contamination diagnostics, with a controlled coverage ablation tracing over-correction to incomplete exposure estimation (Section~\ref{sec:experiments}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Confounding in LLM Evaluation}
\label{sec:confounding}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig2_ovb_bias}
\caption{Sequential estimation bias ($\hat{\theta}_{\mathrm{seq}} - \theta_{\mathrm{true}}$) versus true contamination susceptibility $c_n$. Pearson $r = 0.86$ ($p < 0.001$) confirms systematic overestimation of contaminated models. Synthetic data: $N = 50$, $I = 100$, $J = 8$.}
\label{fig:ovb_bias}
\end{figure}

The Many-Facet Rasch Model (MFRM) extends classical Rasch measurement to settings with multiple rater facets \citep{linacre1989mfrm, linacre1994mfrm}. Originating in educational measurement, the model treats rater variation as a nuisance facet to be estimated and removed rather than a source of noise to be averaged over. In the LLM evaluation context, where $J$ judges score $N$ models across $I$ items, the MFRM specifies
%
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:mfrm}
\log \frac{P(Y_{nij} \geq k \mid \theta_n, b_i, d_j, \tau_k)}{P(Y_{nij} < k \mid \theta_n, b_i, d_j, \tau_k)} = \theta_n - b_i - d_j - \tau_k,
\end{equation}
%
where $Y_{nij}$ is the ordinal rating by judge $j$ for model $n$ on item $i$, $\theta_n$ is the ability of model $n$ ($n = 1, \ldots, N$), $b_i$ is the difficulty of item $i$ ($i = 1, \ldots, I$), $d_j$ is the severity of judge $j$ ($j = 1, \ldots, J$), and $\tau_k$ is the threshold for category $k$. All facets reside on a common logit scale, so model ability is directly comparable after adjustment for item difficulty and judge severity. \citet{jiao2025mfrm_llm} applied this framework to compare ten LLM raters with human experts, showing that MFRM separates genuine ability from systematic judge effects in multi-judge panels.

However, standard MFRM assumes that all systematic sources of score variation are captured by the modeled facets. This assumption breaks down when some models have been exposed to benchmark items during training \citep{dcr2025}. Such contamination inflates scores on exposed items through memorization rather than genuine ability. We represent this effect as an interaction $c_n \cdot e_i$, where $c_n \geq 0$ is the contamination susceptibility of model $n$ and $e_i \in [0, 1]$ is the exposure level of item $i$. The complete data-generating process becomes
%
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:full}
\eta_{nijk} = \theta_n - b_i - d_j - \tau_k + c_n \cdot e_i.
\end{equation}


When a practitioner fits the standard MFRM (Eq.~\ref{eq:mfrm}) to data generated by Eq.~\ref{eq:full}, the contamination term is absent from the estimated model. Its influence does not vanish but is absorbed into the estimated parameters $\tilde{\theta}_n$, $\tilde{b}_i$, and $\tilde{d}_j$, which distorts all downstream inferences.

The consequence of this omission can be stated precisely.

\begin{observation}[Omitted Variable Bias in MFRM]
\label{obs:ovb}
Let the full model be $\eta_{nijk} = \theta_n - b_i - d_j - \tau_k + c_n \cdot e_i$ and the short model omit the contamination term: $\tilde{\eta}_{nijk} = \tilde{\theta}_n - \tilde{b}_i - \tilde{d}_j - \tau_k$. Under a linear approximation to the ordinal logit link \citep{wooldridge2010, angrist2009mostly},\footnote{The OVB formula is exact for linear models. We apply it to the MFRM linear predictor $\eta$, which is an approximation. Synthetic validation confirms the approximation quality: the Pearson correlation between the predicted bias direction and empirical bias reaches $r = 0.86$ ($p < 0.001$), indicating that the linear result accurately characterizes bias direction and relative magnitude.}
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:ovb}
\mathrm{plim}(\tilde{\theta}_n - \theta_n) = \gamma_n \cdot c_n,
\end{equation}
where $\gamma_n$ depends on the projection of the exposure vector $(e_i)$ onto the design matrix of the short model (explicit form in Appendix~\ref{app:gamma}). The bias is non-zero whenever $\mathrm{Cov}(d_j,\, c_n \cdot e_i) \neq 0$. This captures the confounding component; in the nonlinear MFRM, a specification bias from omitting $c_n \cdot e_i$ persists even at zero covariance (Section~\ref{sec:experiments}).
\end{observation}

G-MFRM uses the full nonlinear likelihood; the linear approximation serves only as directional motivation. Monte Carlo simulation confirms that bias magnitude is approximately constant across confounding levels $\rho$ (Figure~\ref{fig:bias_vs_rho}), indicating that omission of $c_n \cdot e_i$---not its correlation with $d_j$---is the primary driver. Joint estimation reduces the bias slope by ${\sim}$69\% at all $\rho$ levels. Neither sequential ordering resolves this: bias-first approaches \citep{cyclicjudge2026} absorb contamination into $\hat{\theta}$, while contamination-first approaches \citep{dcr2025} misattribute judge severity as contamination ($r = 0.86$, $p < 0.001$; Figure~\ref{fig:ovb_bias}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig4_bias_vs_rho}
\caption{Sequential estimation bias is approximately constant across confounding levels $\rho$, confirming that omission of the contamination term---not its correlation with judge severity---drives the bias. Joint G-MFRM reduces the bias slope by ${\sim}$69\% at all $\rho$ levels. Synthetic data: $N = 50$, $I = 100$, $J = 8$.}
\label{fig:bias_vs_rho}
\end{figure}

